/* ===== The Baku Startup (B2–C1, Business English) and The Debate Club (B2–C2) ===== */
/* question format: [text, options (correct first), explanation, meta?] */

STORIES.business = { title: "The Baku Startup", level: "B2–C1", unit: "Meeting",
units: [
{ id: "team", name: "The Team", az: "Komanda", ru: "Команда", emoji: "👥", img: null,
  note: "Leyla, Rashad and Aysel have just quit their corporate jobs to launch Çay Bridge, an app that connects tea growers in Lankaran and Astara with independent cafés across Europe. Leyla, the CEO, is in charge of strategy and fundraising. Rashad, the CTO, is responsible for building the platform, while Aysel heads up sales and partnerships. On Monday morning they sit down in a tiny co-working space near Fountain Square. “Let's get everyone on the same page,” Leyla says. “We're a lean team, so we all wear many hats, but each of us has to own our area.” Rashad nods: “I report to you, but I'd like a free hand on the tech side.” Aysel laughs: “And I'll be the one on the road, pitching Caspian tea to baristas in Berlin.”",
  qs: [
["What does Çay Bridge do?", ["It connects Caspian tea growers with European cafés.", "It sells coffee machines in Baku.", "It trains baristas in Berlin."], "Çay Bridge is an app that connects tea growers in Lankaran and Astara with independent cafés across Europe."],
["Who is responsible for building the platform?", ["Rashad, the CTO", "Leyla, the CEO", "Aysel, the head of sales"], "The CTO (Chief Technology Officer) is in charge of technology. Rashad is responsible for building the platform."],
["“We all wear many hats” means…", ["each person has several different roles", "the team has a strict dress code", "everyone does exactly the same job"], "To wear many hats = to do several different jobs at once. Very common in small startups."],
["Rashad is responsible ___ building the platform.", ["for", "of", "to"], "The collocation is responsible for + noun / -ing: responsible for building, responsible for sales.", {"t": "type"}],
["“Let's get everyone on the same page” means…", ["let's make sure we all understand and agree on the plan", "let's all read the same report", "let's sit in the same room"], "To be on the same page = to share the same understanding of a situation or plan."],
["Riddle: I land in your inbox with a subject line, and I can begin with “Dear Sir” or “Hi there”. Next, the team learns to write ___.", ["emails", "contracts", "charts"], "Emails! Next meeting: how to choose the right tone, formal or informal."]]},

{ id: "emails", name: "Emails & Tone", az: "E-poçtlar və ton", ru: "Письма и тон", emoji: "📧", img: null,
  note: "Aysel needs to write to two people today. The first is Klaus Weber, the purchasing manager of a café chain in Hamburg, whom she has never met. The second is Tom, a London barista she knows from a trade fair. Her draft to Klaus begins: “Hey Klaus! Wanna try our tea?” Leyla winces. “Too casual. With a new contact, go formal: ‘Dear Mr Weber, I am writing to introduce…’ and end with ‘Kind regards’. Save ‘Hi Tom, just touching base’ and ‘Cheers’ for people you already know.” She adds two more rules: “Put your key request in the first two lines, and never send an angry email. Sleep on it first.” By lunchtime Aysel has sent both emails, and the first reply has arrived: “Please find attached our requirements.”",
  qs: [
["Why does Leyla say Aysel's first draft is wrong?", ["It is too informal for a new contact.", "It is far too long.", "It is full of spelling mistakes."], "“Hey Klaus! Wanna…” is fine for a friend, but too casual for someone you have never met."],
["Which opening is best for a first email to Mr Weber?", ["Dear Mr Weber, I am writing to introduce our company.", "Hey Klaus! What's up?", "Yo Mr Weber, check this out."], "A first email to a new business contact: Dear + title + surname, then I am writing to… ."],
["“Just touching base” means…", ["just contacting you briefly to check in", "just playing a game of baseball", "just finishing the project"], "To touch base = to make brief contact with someone to see how things are going."],
["Please find ___ our price list.", ["attached", "attaching", "attach"], "A fixed formal phrase: Please find attached… (= the file is attached to this email). “Enclosed” is also accepted.", {"t": "type", "alt": ["enclosed"]}],
["Which sign-off is the most formal?", ["Kind regards", "Cheers", "See ya"], "Kind regards / Best regards are neutral-formal. Cheers is informal; See ya is only for friends."],
["Riddle: I happen around a table or on Zoom, I have an agenda, and people try to reach a deal in me. Next, the team has a ___.", ["meeting", "holiday", "party"], "A meeting! Klaus Weber is flying to Baku to negotiate."]]},

{ id: "negotiation", name: "Meetings & Negotiation", az: "İclaslar və danışıqlar", ru: "Встречи и переговоры", emoji: "🤝", img: null,
  note: "Klaus Weber flies to Baku for a meeting. Leyla opens: “Shall we get down to business? First on the agenda is pricing.” Klaus asks for a 20% discount. Leyla doesn't say no; she plays for time: “That's an interesting proposal. Let me run it by my team.” After a short break she comes back with a counter-offer: “We could go as far as 10%, provided that you sign a two-year contract.” Klaus asks for a ballpark figure for the first year's volume, and Aysel suggests about two tonnes. “I think we're on the same wavelength,” Klaus smiles, “but I can't commit today. Let's meet each other halfway: 12%, with an option to review.” Everyone shakes hands. Leyla sums up: “So, to recap, we've agreed in principle.”",
  qs: [
["What does Klaus ask for at first?", ["a 20% discount", "free delivery", "a share in the company"], "Klaus asks for a 20% discount. Leyla answers with a counter-offer of 10%."],
["A “ballpark figure” is…", ["a rough estimate", "an exact final price", "a sports result"], "A ballpark figure = an approximate number, not an exact one. “Can you give me a ballpark figure?”"],
["“Let's meet each other halfway” means…", ["let's compromise", "let's walk to the middle of the room", "let's stop the negotiation"], "To meet someone halfway = each side gives up something to reach an agreement. 10% + 20% → 12%."],
["We could offer 10%, ___ that you sign a two-year contract.", ["provided", "unless", "despite"], "Provided (that) = only if. It sets a condition. “Providing” is also accepted.", {"t": "type", "alt": ["providing"]}],
["Which phrase is used to summarise at the end of a meeting?", ["So, to recap…", "Shall we get down to business?", "First on the agenda is…"], "To recap = to repeat the main points briefly. The other two phrases are used at the start of a meeting."],
["Riddle: I go up, I go down, I can “plummet” or “level off”, and investors love me on a slide. Next, the team studies numbers and ___.", ["charts", "recipes", "poems"], "Charts! Next meeting: describing numbers and trends."]]},

{ id: "trends", name: "Numbers & Trends", az: "Rəqəmlər və tendensiyalar", ru: "Цифры и тенденции", emoji: "📈", img: null,
  note: "Rashad prepares a chart for future investors. “Look at this line,” he explains. “In January we had 40 cafés on the platform. Sales rose steadily until April, then soared by 150% in May after the Berlin food fair. In July they plummeted, because a heatwave hit Europe and nobody wanted hot tea. Since September they have levelled off at around 200 orders a week.” Leyla stops him. “Don't hide the dip. Investors will spot it anyway. Explain it and show the recovery.” Aysel adds a pie chart: Germany accounts for 45% of revenue, followed by Poland with 20%. “Our customer acquisition cost has fallen by a third,” Rashad says, “and our burn rate is under control.” “Good,” says Leyla. “The numbers tell the story. We just narrate it.”",
  qs: [
["What happened to sales in July?", ["They plummeted because of a heatwave.", "They soared after a food fair.", "They levelled off."], "In July a heatwave hit Europe, nobody wanted hot tea, and sales plummeted."],
["“To plummet” means…", ["to fall very quickly and a lot", "to rise slowly", "to stay the same"], "Plummet / plunge / slump = fall sharply. The opposite: soar / rocket / shoot up."],
["Since September, sales ___ at around 200 orders a week.", ["have levelled off", "levelled off since", "are levelling off from"], "Since + a point in time → present perfect: have levelled off (= stopped rising or falling and stayed stable)."],
["Sales rose ___ 150% in May.", ["by", "with", "on"], "Rise by = the size of the change (+150%). Rise to = the final level (to 500 orders).", {"t": "type"}],
["Germany ___ 45% of revenue.", ["accounts for", "counts on", "takes over"], "To account for = to form a part of a total. Germany accounts for 45% of revenue."],
["Riddle: The phone rings at 7 a.m. It's London, and the voice is not happy. Something has gone wrong. Next comes a ___ call.", ["crisis", "birthday", "holiday"], "A crisis call! Next meeting: diplomatic language under pressure."]]},

{ id: "crisis", name: "The Crisis Call", az: "Böhran zəngi", ru: "Кризисный звонок", emoji: "📞", img: null,
  note: "James Hart, Çay Bridge's partner in London, is furious: a shipment of 300 kilos arrived two weeks late, and three cafés have cancelled their orders. Rashad wants to say, “It's not our fault. Customs were slow!” Leyla takes the phone instead. “James, I completely understand your frustration, and I'm sorry for the inconvenience. It seems there may have been a delay at customs, but we should have warned you earlier. What we'd suggest is a 15% credit on your next order.” James softens: “I appreciate that, but I'm afraid we can't afford another delay.” “Absolutely. We'll keep you in the loop every step of the way,” Leyla promises. After the call she tells Rashad: “Never point fingers in a crisis. Acknowledge, apologise, offer a solution, and don't cut corners on shipping again.”",
  qs: [
["Why is James angry?", ["A shipment arrived two weeks late.", "The tea tasted bad.", "Çay Bridge raised its prices."], "The shipment arrived two weeks late, and three cafés cancelled their orders."],
["Which sentence is the most diplomatic?", ["It seems there may have been a delay at customs.", "Customs ruined everything.", "It's not our fault at all."], "It seems… / there may have been… soften the message: no blame, no strong claims."],
["“I'm afraid we can't afford another delay.” Here, “I'm afraid”…", ["softens bad news politely", "means James is scared", "shows that James is joking"], "In business, I'm afraid… = a polite way to say something negative. It has nothing to do with fear."],
["We'll keep you in the ___ every step of the way.", ["loop", "circle", "line"], "To keep someone in the loop = to keep them informed about what is happening.", {"t": "type"}],
["“Don't cut corners” means…", ["don't do things cheaply or carelessly to save time or money", "don't turn left too fast", "don't redesign the office"], "To cut corners = to do something badly or cheaply to save time or money, often causing problems later."],
["Riddle: Ten minutes, one slide deck, and a room of investors in a glass tower by the Gulf. Next stop: the pitch in ___.", ["Dubai", "Hamburg", "London"], "Dubai! Next meeting: the investor pitch."]]},

{ id: "pitch", name: "The Pitch in Dubai", az: "Dubayda təqdimat", ru: "Питч в Дубае", emoji: "🏙️", img: null,
  note: "In a glass tower overlooking the Gulf, Leyla has ten minutes in front of five investors. She opens with a hook: “Every morning, a café in Berlin serves tea that has travelled 6,000 kilometres through four middlemen. We cut that chain down to one click.” She walks them through the problem, the solution, the traction and, finally, the ask: “We're raising 500,000 dollars for a 15% stake.” One investor, Ms Al Mansouri, pushes back: “What's your competitive edge? Anyone can build an app.” Leyla is ready: “Our edge isn't the app. It's the relationships. We've signed exclusive deals with 60 tea farms. That's a barrier to entry no one can copy overnight.” The room goes quiet. Then Ms Al Mansouri leans forward: “All right. Let's talk numbers.”",
  qs: [
["How does Leyla open her pitch?", ["with a hook: a striking fact about the tea's journey", "with a long list of numbers", "with a joke about investors"], "A hook is an opening that grabs attention: 6,000 km and four middlemen, cut down to one click."],
["“The ask” in a pitch is…", ["the money you want and what you offer in return", "the first question from the audience", "the company's address"], "The ask = what you are asking investors for: here, 500,000 dollars for a 15% stake."],
["A “competitive edge” is…", ["an advantage over your competitors", "a sharp knife", "a risky strategy"], "Competitive edge = something that makes you better than your rivals. For Çay Bridge: exclusive deals with 60 farms."],
["We're raising 500,000 dollars for a 15% ___ in the company.", ["stake", "steak", "stack"], "A stake = a share of ownership in a company. Careful: steak is meat! “Share” is also accepted.", {"t": "type", "alt": ["share"]}],
["Why does Ms Al Mansouri doubt the app?", ["Anyone can build an app.", "Apps are too expensive.", "She dislikes technology."], "“Anyone can build an app” = the technology alone is no barrier to entry. Leyla answers: the relationships are."],
["Riddle: I'm signed at the end, I'm full of “shall” and “must”, and lawyers read every line of me. Last meeting: the deal and the ___.", ["contract", "menu", "invitation"], "The contract! Last meeting: modal verbs of obligation and legal phrasing."]]},

{ id: "contract", name: "The Deal & the Contract", az: "Saziş və müqavilə", ru: "Сделка и контракт", emoji: "✍️", img: null,
  note: "Two weeks later, the term sheet arrives from Dubai. Leyla and a lawyer, Mr Hasanov, go through it line by line. “The Company shall provide quarterly financial reports,” he reads. “In a contract, ‘shall’ means a legal obligation: you must do it.” Clause 7 says the founders may not sell their shares for three years without the investors' consent. “Here ‘may not’ means it's prohibited,” he explains. “But look at clause 9: you don't have to hire a CFO until next year. That's optional for now.” Leyla wants to change one point: “We'd need the right to veto any sale of the company.” After two rounds of edits, both sides sign. The deal is done, and the champagne is replaced by Lankaran tea. “Now,” says Aysel, “the real work begins.”",
  qs: [
["In a contract, “The Company shall provide reports” means…", ["the company is legally obliged to provide reports", "the company might provide reports", "the company provided reports in the past"], "In legal English, shall = must. It expresses an obligation, not the future."],
["“You don't have to hire a CFO until next year” means…", ["it isn't necessary yet", "it is forbidden", "it is required now"], "Don't have to = no obligation, it's optional. Compare: mustn't / may not = it's prohibited."],
["Founders ___ sell their shares for three years without consent. (= it is prohibited)", ["may not", "don't have to", "needn't"], "May not / must not = prohibition. Don't have to and needn't = it isn't necessary, which is completely different."],
["A ___ sheet sets out the main conditions of an investment before the final contract.", ["term", "time", "word"], "A term sheet = a short document listing the key terms of a deal before the full contract is written.", {"t": "type"}],
["The closest meaning of “consent” is…", ["permission", "payment", "complaint"], "Consent = permission or agreement. Without the investors' consent = without their permission."],
["Final: what is Çay Bridge?", ["an app connecting Caspian tea growers with European cafés", "a tea shop in Dubai", "a law firm in London"], "From the first team meeting to the signed contract: Çay Bridge connects Caspian tea with Europe. Congratulations, you've closed the deal!"]]}
]};

STORIES.debate = { title: "The Debate Club", level: "B2–C2", unit: "Round",
units: [
{ id: "rules", name: "How Debating Works", az: "Debat necə işləyir", ru: "Как устроены дебаты", emoji: "🎙️", img: null,
  note: "Welcome to the Caspian Open, an international student debate tournament held in Baku. Teams have arrived from Tokyo, Nairobi and Berlin to face the home team, Nigar and Farid. At the opening ceremony, the chief adjudicator, Dr Ellen Hart, explains the format. “Each round has a motion, a statement to be debated. The proposition argues for it; the opposition argues against it. You won't choose your side: it's decided by a coin toss fifteen minutes before the round. Every speaker builds arguments but, just as crucially, responds to the other side. That's called rebuttal. You may also offer a point of information, a short question during an opponent's speech, though they're entitled to decline it.” Nigar whispers: “So, in other words, we may have to defend things we don't even believe.” “Exactly,” Farid grins. “That's what makes it a skill, not just an opinion.”",
  qs: [
["Which side argues FOR the motion?", ["the proposition", "the opposition", "the adjudicators"], "The proposition (or government) supports the motion; the opposition argues against it."],
["“Rebuttal” is…", ["responding to and undermining the other side's arguments", "the opening statement of a team", "the judges' final score"], "Rebuttal = answering the opponents' arguments and showing why they are weak or wrong."],
["How are the sides decided?", ["by a coin toss before the round", "the teams choose", "the judges vote"], "A coin toss fifteen minutes before the round. That's why debaters must be ready to argue either side."],
["A point of ___ is a short question offered during an opponent's speech.", ["information", "order", "view"], "A point of information (POI) = a brief question or comment offered during the other side's speech. The speaker may accept or decline it.", {"t": "type"}],
["“So, in other words…” is a discourse marker used to…", ["rephrase or summarise an idea", "introduce a contrast", "give an example"], "In other words / that is to say / to put it another way = rephrasing. For contrast: however, nevertheless."],
["Riddle: I live in your pocket, I count your likes, and I'm accused of stealing your sleep. The first motion is about ___ media.", ["social", "mass", "print"], "Social media! Round 1: does it do more harm than good?"]]},

{ id: "social", name: "Round 1: Social Media", az: "1-ci raund: sosial media", ru: "Раунд 1: соцсети", emoji: "📱", img: null,
  note: "Motion: “This House believes that social media does more harm than good.” Berlin propose. Greta opens: “Admittedly, social media connects people. But at what cost? Studies suggest a link between heavy use and anxiety among teenagers, and algorithms tend to reward outrage rather than accuracy.” Baku oppose. Nigar stands up: “Granted, there are risks. Yet it was social media that allowed small businesses in Ganja to reach customers in Canada, and ordinary citizens to document injustice in real time. The problem is arguably not the tool itself, but how it is regulated.” Farid adds a sharp rebuttal: “The proposition has shown correlation, not causation. Does anxiety lead to more scrolling, or does scrolling lead to anxiety?” Greta replies that, either way, platforms profit from the cycle. The judges note that both teams hedged their claims carefully, which is a hallmark of mature debating.",
  qs: [
["Which word does Greta use to admit a point before arguing against it?", ["Admittedly", "Consequently", "Furthermore"], "Admittedly / granted / it's true that… = concession: you accept part of the other view, then counter it."],
["“Studies suggest a link…” is an example of…", ["hedging: making a claim cautiously", "exaggeration", "a rhetorical question"], "Suggest, tend to, arguably, may = hedging. It makes claims more precise and harder to attack."],
["“It was social media that allowed small businesses to reach customers” is…", ["a cleft sentence used for emphasis", "a passive sentence", "a conditional sentence"], "It + be + X + that/who… = an it-cleft. It puts the focus on X: social media, not something else."],
["___, there are risks. Yet the benefits are far greater.", ["Granted", "Therefore", "Moreover"], "Granted = I accept that… Then yet / however / but introduces the counter-argument. “Admittedly” is also accepted.", {"t": "type", "alt": ["admittedly"]}],
["Farid's point “correlation, not causation” means…", ["two things happening together doesn't prove that one causes the other", "the statistics are invented", "social media definitely causes anxiety"], "Correlation = two things appear together. Causation = one actually causes the other. A classic rebuttal tool."],
["Riddle: I have four wheels, I honk in traffic jams, and some cities want me out of their old streets. Round 2 is about banning ___.", ["cars", "tourists", "bicycles"], "Cars! Round 2: should city centres be car-free?"]]},

{ id: "cars", name: "Round 2: Car-Free Centres", az: "2-ci raund: avtomobilsiz mərkəzlər", ru: "Раунд 2: центры без машин", emoji: "🚗", img: null,
  note: "Motion: “This House would ban cars from city centres.” Nairobi propose, using Baku's own Old City as an example. Amani argues: “Walk through Icherisheher and you'll notice that traffic is already heavily restricted. The streets are calmer, cleaner and arguably better for small businesses. Now imagine extending that to the whole centre. Wouldn't we all breathe more easily?” Tokyo oppose. Haruto responds: “That's all very well for tourists, but what about the elderly, people with disabilities, or a shopkeeper who needs deliveries every morning? A blanket ban would hit the most vulnerable hardest.” Yuki then suggests a middle ground: congestion charges, more pedestrian streets and far better public transport. The judges praise Haruto for a compelling counter-argument and Amani for her vivid rhetorical questions, but note that neither team offered hard evidence on air quality.",
  qs: [
["Which place does Amani use as an example?", ["Icherisheher, Baku's Old City", "a shopping street in Tokyo", "Berlin's airport"], "Amani uses Icherisheher, where car traffic is already heavily restricted."],
["“Wouldn't we all breathe more easily?” is a rhetorical question. It…", ["makes a point rather than expecting an answer", "asks the judges for information", "shows that the speaker is unsure"], "A rhetorical question doesn't need an answer: the answer (“Yes, we would!”) is obvious. It pulls the audience in."],
["“That's all very well for tourists, but…” is used to…", ["accept a point partly and then object to it", "agree completely", "change the subject"], "That's all very well, but… = yes, in theory, but there is a serious problem. A polite but firm objection."],
["A ___ ban would hit the most vulnerable hardest. (= total, covering everything)", ["blanket", "pillow", "sheet"], "A blanket ban = a ban that applies to everything and everyone, with no exceptions. “Total” is also accepted.", {"t": "type", "alt": ["total", "complete"]}],
["Which collocation is natural?", ["a compelling argument", "a pushing argument", "a forcing argument"], "Compelling / persuasive / convincing argument. The opposite: a weak / flawed / unconvincing argument."],
["Riddle: I write essays, drive trucks and help diagnose diseases, but I have no body. Some say I'll take your job; others say I'll give you a new one. Round 3 is about ___.", ["AI", "tourism", "homework"], "Artificial intelligence! Round 3: will AI create more jobs than it destroys?"]]},

{ id: "ai", name: "Round 3: AI and Jobs", az: "3-cü raund: süni intellekt və iş yerləri", ru: "Раунд 3: ИИ и рабочие места", emoji: "🤖", img: null,
  note: "Motion: “This House believes that AI will create more jobs than it destroys.” Baku propose against Berlin. Farid draws on history: “Never before has a technology reduced the total amount of work. The power loom, the computer, the internet: each destroyed some jobs and created far more. AI is likely to follow the same pattern. Prompt engineers, AI auditors, data ethicists: none of these roles existed a decade ago.” Lukas from Berlin is unconvinced: “What my opponent fails to mention is the speed. Previous revolutions unfolded over generations; this one is unfolding in a matter of years. A fifty-year-old translator cannot simply retrain overnight. And even if new jobs do emerge, will they go to the same people who lost theirs?” In her feedback, the judge commends Farid's use of inversion and Lukas's pseudo-cleft, but observes that both sides relied on predictions which, by their very nature, remain contestable.",
  qs: [
["“Never before has a technology reduced the total amount of work” uses…", ["inversion after a negative adverbial, for emphasis", "a question form by mistake", "the passive voice"], "Negative adverbial (never before, rarely, seldom, not only) at the start → auxiliary + subject: has a technology. Formal and emphatic."],
["What is Lukas's main counter-argument?", ["the change is too fast for workers to adapt", "AI is too expensive to use", "history proves that AI will fail"], "Lukas accepts the historical pattern but attacks the speed: generations then, years now."],
["“What my opponent fails to mention is the speed” is…", ["a pseudo-cleft (wh-cleft) sentence", "a reported question", "a third conditional"], "What + clause + be + focus = pseudo-cleft. It delays and highlights the key point: the speed."],
["Rarely ___ a technology spread so quickly. (inversion)", ["has", "it has", "is"], "Rarely + auxiliary + subject: Rarely has a technology spread so quickly. “Had” is also accepted.", {"t": "type", "alt": ["had"]}],
["AI is ___ to follow the same pattern. (a hedged prediction)", ["likely", "certain", "bound"], "Likely to = probable, not guaranteed: a hedge. Certain to / bound to overclaim and are easy to attack."],
["Riddle: I'm a walled city, a floating city, a world heritage site, and millions of visitors with cameras love me to death. Round 4 is about ___.", ["tourism", "architecture", "pollution"], "Tourism! Round 4: does it help heritage sites more than it harms them?"]]},

{ id: "heritage", name: "Round 4: Tourism and Heritage", az: "4-cü raund: turizm və irs", ru: "Раунд 4: туризм и наследие", emoji: "🏛️", img: null,
  note: "Motion: “This House believes that tourism helps heritage sites more than it harms them.” Tokyo propose; Nairobi oppose. Yuki begins: “Heritage costs money. Ticket revenue and visitor spending help fund the restoration of Icherisheher's caravanserais and the Maiden Tower. Without tourists, many sites would simply fall into disrepair, and local craftspeople would lose their livelihoods.” Wanjiru counters with Venice: “The historic centre of Venice now has fewer than 50,000 residents, yet it receives tens of millions of visits a year. Rents have soared, locals have been priced out, and the city has even introduced an entry fee for day-trippers. Is a city still a heritage site when hardly anyone lives there?” Yuki concedes the point but insists that this is a failure of management, not of tourism itself. The judges describe it as the most finely balanced round of the tournament.",
  qs: [
["According to Yuki, how does tourism help heritage?", ["It funds restoration and supports local craftspeople.", "It keeps visitors away from old buildings.", "It replaces old buildings with hotels."], "Ticket revenue and visitor spending pay for restoration and support craftspeople's livelihoods."],
["“Locals have been priced out” means…", ["housing has become too expensive for them, so they have had to leave", "locals were given a discount", "locals now sell the tickets"], "To be priced out (of a place/market) = to be unable to afford it any more because prices have risen."],
["“To fall into disrepair” means…", ["to become damaged because nobody maintains it", "to become more popular", "to be rebuilt"], "Fall into disrepair = slowly break down through neglect. A common collocation for buildings."],
["Yuki ___ the point but argues that it is a failure of management.", ["concedes", "conceals", "concludes"], "To concede a point = to admit that the opponent is right about something. “Accepts” and “admits” are also accepted.", {"t": "type", "alt": ["accepts", "admits", "grants"]}],
["Wanjiru's question “Is a city still a heritage site when hardly anyone lives there?” mainly serves to…", ["challenge the idea that heritage is only about buildings", "ask the judges for statistics", "show that she agrees with Tokyo"], "A rhetorical question that reframes the debate: heritage is also living people and culture, not just stones."],
["Riddle: Teachers give me, students sigh at me, and I follow you home every evening. Round 5 asks: should ___ be abolished?", ["homework", "exams", "uniforms"], "Homework! The semi-final: should homework be abolished?"]]},

{ id: "homework", name: "Round 5: Homework", az: "5-ci raund: ev tapşırığı", ru: "Раунд 5: домашнее задание", emoji: "📚", img: null,
  note: "Motion: “This House would abolish homework.” In the semi-final, Baku propose against Tokyo. Nigar argues: “Not only does homework widen the gap between children whose parents can help them and those whose parents cannot, but it also eats into time for sleep, play and family. Finland, often cited as a high-performing system, is known for setting relatively little of it.” Haruto replies calmly: “Be that as it may, practice is how skills become automatic. The issue is not homework per se, but pointless homework. Abolishing it altogether would be throwing the baby out with the bathwater.” He then cites research suggesting that homework benefits older students considerably more than younger ones. Nigar seizes on this in her reply speech: “So my opponent concedes that for primary pupils, the case for homework is weak at best.” The adjudicators split 2–1 in favour of Baku. They're through to the final.",
  qs: [
["“Not only does homework widen the gap…, but it also…” Why is the word order inverted?", ["A sentence starting with “Not only” needs auxiliary + subject.", "Because it is a question.", "Because homework is plural."], "Not only at the start of a clause triggers inversion: Not only does homework widen…, but it also…"],
["“Be that as it may” means…", ["even if that is true", "because that is true", "that is impossible"], "Be that as it may = even so / nevertheless. A formal way to concede and then disagree."],
["“Throwing the baby out with the bathwater” means…", ["getting rid of something valuable while removing something bad", "washing children carefully", "solving a problem completely"], "The idiom warns against rejecting a whole thing because of one bad part: remove pointless homework, keep useful practice."],
["The issue is not homework ___ se, but pointless homework.", ["per", "by", "in"], "Per se (Latin) = in itself. Not homework per se = not homework as such.", {"t": "type"}],
["“The case for homework is weak at best” means…", ["even viewed most favourably, it is weak", "it is a very strong case", "it was weak before but is better now"], "At best = in the most optimistic view. Weak at best = weak, or possibly even worse. A sharp rebuttal phrase."],
["Riddle: Two teams left, one trophy, and a panel of judges who will tell you exactly what you did wrong. Next comes the ___.", ["final", "break", "opening"], "The final! Baku meet Nairobi, and the judges give their feedback."]]},

{ id: "final", name: "The Final", az: "Final", ru: "Финал", emoji: "🏆", img: null,
  note: "The final pits Baku against Nairobi on the motion “This House regrets the rise of the gig economy.” The hall is packed. Afterwards, Dr Hart delivers the judges' feedback. “Both teams were outstanding. Nairobi, your case was meticulously structured and Amani's rebuttal was devastating. At times, however, you overstated your claims: words like ‘always’ and ‘everyone’ are a gift to your opponents. Baku, what struck us most was your engagement. You took on Nairobi's strongest argument, not their weakest. Seldom have we seen a concession turned into an attack so precisely. On balance, and by the narrowest of margins, this debate goes to Baku.” Nigar and Farid can hardly believe it. Shaking hands with Amani, Nigar says: “Honestly, you could just as easily have won.” Amani smiles: “Next year, in Nairobi. Rematch.”",
  qs: [
["What criticism does Dr Hart give Nairobi?", ["They sometimes overstated their claims.", "Their speeches were too short.", "They ignored the time limit."], "Overstating = claiming more than you can prove. Absolute words make this easy to spot."],
["“What struck us most was your engagement.” The structure “What… was…” is used to…", ["focus attention on the key point", "ask a question", "report someone's words"], "A pseudo-cleft: it builds suspense and puts the focus at the end: your engagement."],
["“Seldom have we seen a concession turned into an attack so precisely” means…", ["we have rarely seen this", "we often see this", "we never want to see this"], "Seldom = rarely. Seldom at the start → inversion: Seldom have we seen…"],
["On ___, and by the narrowest of margins, this debate goes to Baku.", ["balance", "weight", "scale"], "On balance = after considering all the arguments on both sides. A classic phrase for verdicts and conclusions.", {"t": "type"}],
["Why do absolute words like “always” and “everyone” weaken an argument?", ["One counter-example is enough to disprove them.", "Judges don't understand them.", "They are grammatically incorrect."], "If you say “everyone”, your opponent only needs one exception. Hedged language (often, many, most) is harder to attack."],
["Final: which skill did the judges reward most throughout the tournament?", ["engaging fairly with the other side's strongest arguments", "speaking as fast as possible", "never conceding any point"], "From Round 1 to the final: hedge your claims, concede wisely, and answer the strongest version of the other side. Congratulations, champion debater!"]]}
]};

